{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790424764,"nonce":"vWeyx9PG5ODd9uJr/l/ydJsJaaA=","message":"Stage 2 landed in 12b72bb.\n\n- `object_layout::Layout` has one table per pointer width (`LP64`, `ILP32`), plus `HOST`, `for_triple` and `for_pointer_bytes`. The existing constants read `Layout::HOST`, so the JIT is unchanged.\n- `Layout::measured()` computes the build's real layout with offset_of!; the closure's Vec data pointer is found by value, since Rust does not fix Vec's field order.\n- Native check: the host table equals measured(). Wasm32 check: `wlift_layout_probe` (wasm32 + aot_runtime only) writes measured() out, and tests/wasm_runtime_fresh.rs links the object, calls it under wasmtime and compares against ILP32, listing each stale entry.\n- `WliftAotMethodDesc` joined the table (16 bytes on wasm32, 32 on 64-bit); emit_aot_bootstrap_main reads it for its ISA's pointer width instead of literal 32/8/16/24/25.\n\nFor stage 3: llvm_backend still glob-imports the host constants. Its AOT mode has to take a `Layout` chosen from the target triple and read offsets from that. The bootstrap's modvar, const, symbol and closure slot tables use 8-byte strides; they hold u64 Values/ids, so the stride is the same on both widths, but check each when porting the bootstrap.","files":null}]}